WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.141 --> 00:00:04.357
- When Oasis came along, we
didn't have the internet,

00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:06.138
you know, if we'd have had the internet,

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:08.074
we'd have been bigger than the universe.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.081
I don't live to work, I work to live,

00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:11.436
and what's the point of being a rock star

00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:14.022
if you can't just bum around for a year?

00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:15.839
The gigs and the tours and
all that, you should be

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:17.958
having fun on those things
or what the fuck are we

00:00:17.958 --> 00:00:20.158
even doing them for, I dunno?

00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:22.537
- He's an English musician,
singer, songwriter,

00:00:22.537 --> 00:00:25.038
and guitarist, he was
the vocalist, songwriter,

00:00:25.038 --> 00:00:27.461
and lead guitarist for
the rock band Oasis,

00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:30.119
many have praised his
songwriting, with George Martin

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:33.121
calling him the finest
songwriter of his generation,

00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:35.681
he's Noel Gallagher, and
here's my take on his top 10

00:00:35.681 --> 00:00:38.613
rules for success, and as
always guys, as you're watching

00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:40.661
the videos, if you hear
something that really resonates

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:42.919
with you, please leave it
down in the comments below

00:00:42.919 --> 00:00:45.038
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:45.038 --> 00:00:47.540
as well, also as you are
writing something down,

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.707
it's much more likely to stick
in your head, too, enjoy.

00:00:55.058 --> 00:00:57.475
(epic music)

00:01:04.357 --> 00:01:07.690
- There can't be the
greatest band in the world

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.161
playing in a pub tonight
that nobody's heard of

00:01:10.161 --> 00:01:14.234
with the internet, if you're
great, you going to make it,

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:16.275
you know, that's just a
fact, there can't be any,

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:19.312
well, we didn't make it
because we're working class

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.753
or summat, do you know what I mean?

00:01:20.753 --> 00:01:22.519
Like, if you're writing the
best songs in the world,

00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:24.075
you're going to make it in the end.

00:01:24.075 --> 00:01:27.258
You know, when Oasis came
along, we didn't have

00:01:27.258 --> 00:01:29.758
the internet, you know, if
we'd have had the internet,

00:01:29.758 --> 00:01:32.237
we'd have been bigger than
the universe, you know what

00:01:32.237 --> 00:01:33.775
I mean, we could have reached everybody.

00:01:33.775 --> 00:01:34.852
- Yeah.

00:01:34.852 --> 00:01:37.380
- You know, but we did it,
we were forced to do it

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.097
the hard way, you know,
these new bands have all

00:01:40.097 --> 00:01:42.795
the advantage of, you know, these devices

00:01:42.795 --> 00:01:45.617
that can get you to everybody in the world

00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:48.950
at the click of a button, and, you know,

00:01:49.878 --> 00:01:52.380
all they got to come up
with is, oh, right, yeah,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.079
the difficult bit, is the f-ing songs.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:58.356
(laughs) if you're a
songwriter or a composer,

00:01:58.356 --> 00:02:01.301
you're kind of in it for
life, I enjoy the writing

00:02:01.301 --> 00:02:04.722
although it can be very
frustrating when it's not happening

00:02:04.722 --> 00:02:08.889
but I do enjoy that, I genuinely
don't know what I would do.

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:11.984
- Some people from some
quarters give you steak

00:02:11.984 --> 00:02:14.742
for being, like, a formulaic songwriter,

00:02:14.742 --> 00:02:19.107
but I'd say, Chasing Yesterday
is kind of progressive,

00:02:19.107 --> 00:02:20.547
really, isn't it?

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:23.763
- Some of that, well.
- Relatively speaking.

00:02:23.763 --> 00:02:25.562
- What does that mean,
anyway, what does it mean

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:30.044
and who are some people, the
Guardian, because the Guardian.

00:02:30.044 --> 00:02:32.320
Everybody's got an opinion
anyways, do you know what I mean,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.582
but I don't really
think about it too much,

00:02:35.582 --> 00:02:39.465
if it sounds good to
me, I put it out there,

00:02:39.465 --> 00:02:41.927
I'm not begging people to come and see me

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:45.607
or buy it or anything
like that, if my audience,

00:02:45.607 --> 00:02:48.324
if such a thing exists,
gets it, that's great

00:02:48.324 --> 00:02:49.624
and if they don't, that's great, too,

00:02:49.624 --> 00:02:50.884
you know what I mean, I kind of like it.

00:02:50.884 --> 00:02:54.285
- Creatively, where were
you at at the end of Oasis?

00:02:54.285 --> 00:02:58.452
- Creatively, well, I would
always take a lot of time off

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.546
after big world tours anyway,
do you know what I mean?

00:03:02.546 --> 00:03:05.686
You've got to, if I
was, 'cause each project

00:03:05.686 --> 00:03:09.582
that we used to undertake would
be two years with the tour,

00:03:09.582 --> 00:03:11.544
from the minute you enter
the studio to the end

00:03:11.544 --> 00:03:14.667
of the last gig would be
two years, so you've got to,

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:18.379
if I was working for two
years, I'd have to balance

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:22.561
it out with time off because
I don't, I don't live to work,

00:03:22.561 --> 00:03:25.284
I work to live, and what's
the point of being a rock star

00:03:25.284 --> 00:03:28.163
if you can't just bum around for a year?

00:03:28.163 --> 00:03:29.684
There's no point in working
every day of your life.

00:03:29.684 --> 00:03:31.980
- Do you consider it work,
some people would look at

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.499
your life and think that that work isn't,

00:03:33.499 --> 00:03:36.522
is something, it's your
passion, so it's ...

00:03:36.522 --> 00:03:38.101
It's what you live for.

00:03:38.101 --> 00:03:40.348
- On the one hand, it's my
hobby, do you know what I mean

00:03:40.348 --> 00:03:42.726
and I don't have any other
hobbies but watching football,

00:03:42.726 --> 00:03:47.024
but, you know, like I
say, I couldn't go from

00:03:47.024 --> 00:03:50.961
spending two years on a project
and then six weeks later

00:03:50.961 --> 00:03:52.923
saying, right, well, I'm going
to start something else now,

00:03:52.923 --> 00:03:56.207
there's got to be, now
this is just me personally,

00:03:56.207 --> 00:03:58.146
some people like to work all the time,

00:03:58.146 --> 00:04:02.204
I have to go away and I
don't pick up the guitar

00:04:02.204 --> 00:04:05.164
and something happens
and then I have to feel

00:04:05.164 --> 00:04:07.225
that I really, really miss
it, like I might go and see

00:04:07.225 --> 00:04:09.988
a show from another band
and think, I really want to

00:04:09.988 --> 00:04:12.843
get back on stage, you know,
and then I'll think about

00:04:12.843 --> 00:04:15.845
doing something, we're
all in the music game

00:04:15.845 --> 00:04:18.460
for different reasons,
do you know what I mean?

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.793
And I am not a workaholic, I'm just not.

00:04:22.666 --> 00:04:26.833
I pay for and release my own
records, and I produced it,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.964
I don't have anybody
saying to me, well, I think

00:04:30.964 --> 00:04:35.441
it's a bit long, you know,
or an A and R guy saying,

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:38.148
why does the guitar solo come
in after the first chorus,

00:04:38.148 --> 00:04:40.244
and then there's a big,
do you know what I mean?

00:04:40.244 --> 00:04:43.646
I allow myself now to follow my instincts

00:04:43.646 --> 00:04:46.548
and sometimes they're wrong
and sometimes they're right,

00:04:46.548 --> 00:04:47.546
you know.

00:04:47.546 --> 00:04:50.803
Well, I believe in what
I do, in the same reason

00:04:50.803 --> 00:04:53.868
that I believe in and I'm
comfortable with who I am

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:58.035
and what I wear and how I
look and how I'm perceived,

00:04:59.563 --> 00:05:03.086
I take the writing and
the recording of my music

00:05:03.086 --> 00:05:07.324
very seriously and the rest
of it, I don't take seriously

00:05:07.324 --> 00:05:08.984
at all, I don't take myself seriously,

00:05:08.984 --> 00:05:11.818
I don't take things like
these interviews seriously,

00:05:11.818 --> 00:05:13.843
I would never do an interview
where I'm like, well,

00:05:13.843 --> 00:05:16.961
you know, tell you how I wrote
that song or what it means,

00:05:16.961 --> 00:05:19.044
nobody gives a shit, I
don't care, you know,

00:05:19.044 --> 00:05:23.067
so yeah, I don't take a
great deal of it seriously,

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:26.716
if I'm being honest,
apart from the records,

00:05:26.716 --> 00:05:30.750
they're serious because they last forever.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.322
The gigs and the tours and
all that, you should be

00:05:32.322 --> 00:05:34.625
having fun on doing those
things or what the fuck even

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:36.458
are we doing, I dunno.

00:05:38.288 --> 00:05:40.848
Funny thing about gigs is ...

00:05:40.848 --> 00:05:42.783
There's so many factors
that make a great gig,

00:05:42.783 --> 00:05:44.782
like, I mean, I've come
offstage so many times thinking

00:05:44.782 --> 00:05:48.888
that was awful, for
whatever reason, you know,

00:05:48.888 --> 00:05:50.728
your sound man'll come
in, he was out front,

00:05:50.728 --> 00:05:52.348
to say, that's the best
thing I've ever seen,

00:05:52.348 --> 00:05:55.331
you go, really, or then you'll be offstage

00:05:55.331 --> 00:05:58.748
and think that your band has played great

00:05:59.586 --> 00:06:02.768
and the sound man'll come in
and say, oh, I lost the bass

00:06:02.768 --> 00:06:04.869
for six songs, you're, like, really,

00:06:04.869 --> 00:06:06.930
or the lighting man'll say,
well, yes, but the lights

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.008
didn't work, you know
what I mean, it's kind of,

00:06:09.008 --> 00:06:11.384
I don't really, I don't really judge gigs,

00:06:11.384 --> 00:06:12.985
while I'm doing it, I'm just
in the moment, I'm singing

00:06:12.985 --> 00:06:14.943
the songs, when I come
offstage, I don't like talking

00:06:14.943 --> 00:06:15.947
about it.

00:06:15.947 --> 00:06:18.151
- So when you're writing
a song, I mean, how do you

00:06:18.151 --> 00:06:20.809
put it to, what's the process?

00:06:20.809 --> 00:06:23.923
- I just play the guitar,
I might be playing a set

00:06:23.923 --> 00:06:26.870
of chords for three months
and I will just be fascinated

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.288
by the chords and nothing
will happen and then one day

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:33.649
if I perservere with it
enough, a melody will arrive

00:06:33.649 --> 00:06:35.899
and then lyrics will evolve

00:06:37.528 --> 00:06:40.361
and then to get a band to play it,

00:06:43.228 --> 00:06:47.106
I come from a school of music,
well, we all did in Oasis,

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:49.387
where you just say, right,
it goes like this, you know,

00:06:49.387 --> 00:06:53.554
I don't know, I know the
chord E, I know quite a few

00:06:54.645 --> 00:06:56.605
chords, the major chords
now and the minor chords,

00:06:56.605 --> 00:06:59.529
but when we get into
suspended and augmented things

00:06:59.529 --> 00:07:02.461
and all that, I find that,
I just, I don't know what

00:07:02.461 --> 00:07:06.686
they all are, but, yeah,
it was more the punk ethic,

00:07:06.686 --> 00:07:08.482
it goes like this, just
play along and I'll copy it.

00:07:08.482 --> 00:07:11.444
- You're an independent artist these days,

00:07:11.444 --> 00:07:15.086
what do you enjoy most about
being I guess not releasing

00:07:15.086 --> 00:07:17.768
music on the major labels?

00:07:17.768 --> 00:07:20.810
- I enjoy the lack of money that I make.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.069
(audience laughs)

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:25.902
I enjoy the lack of
record sales, I also enjoy

00:07:26.986 --> 00:07:29.668
the lack, I enjoy the lack of recognition

00:07:29.668 --> 00:07:32.918
and the lack of rewards that I receive.

00:07:34.051 --> 00:07:37.191
'Cause quite frankly my
mantelpiece, it was chock a block

00:07:37.191 --> 00:07:39.786
with the f-ing things, so ....

00:07:39.786 --> 00:07:40.808
(audience laughs)

00:07:40.808 --> 00:07:44.689
It's nice to take a
backseat, I enjoy not working

00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:48.830
with people in record labels
who are in fact, as we all

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.005
know, (bleep), so ...

00:07:51.005 --> 00:07:52.331
(audience laughs)

00:07:52.331 --> 00:07:55.204
It's nice to get away from that mob.

00:07:55.204 --> 00:07:57.445
- I've never felt the need
to be in the public eye

00:07:57.445 --> 00:08:00.226
or the need, even when I
was in Oasis, I was always

00:08:00.226 --> 00:08:04.326
the last person to get on
board to go back to the studio.

00:08:04.326 --> 00:08:08.493
I never really, that never
really, I don't chase my,

00:08:09.931 --> 00:08:12.304
I don't chase fame or success
or anything like that.

00:08:12.304 --> 00:08:15.824
I mean, I was kind of,
I'd taken about a year out

00:08:15.824 --> 00:08:19.241
and, you know, I'd written a lot of songs

00:08:21.187 --> 00:08:25.354
and I'd demo'ed most of them,
and there just came a point

00:08:26.222 --> 00:08:28.345
where I felt I should get in the studio

00:08:28.345 --> 00:08:31.721
but not for any other reason
than to record these songs

00:08:31.721 --> 00:08:34.365
and see what they sounded
like and I didn't really tell

00:08:34.365 --> 00:08:38.562
anybody, I kept it quiet
for a year and a half, and

00:08:38.562 --> 00:08:41.002
it was all very relaxed and
low key, it was just me,

00:08:41.002 --> 00:08:45.169
an engineer, a drummer, and
a part time keyboard player

00:08:46.462 --> 00:08:48.726
who came in and out, and
there was only three of us

00:08:48.726 --> 00:08:50.419
in there and it was done
very kind of leisurely,

00:08:50.419 --> 00:08:54.642
and then, you know, my
wife went and got pregnant,

00:08:54.642 --> 00:08:58.903
so I had six months off
looking after my new son

00:08:58.903 --> 00:09:01.741
and all that, so it was very,
it was quite a leisurely

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:04.360
pace, some might say majestic.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.043
- [Woman] You could have
easily decided to, you know,

00:09:06.043 --> 00:09:09.241
start to retire.
- Well yeah, yeah.

00:09:09.241 --> 00:09:10.483
- [Woman] But you didn't,

00:09:10.483 --> 00:09:11.482
but you're still enjoying making music.

00:09:11.482 --> 00:09:16.019
- I've never lost the sense
of wonder of writing songs,

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:19.481
you know, in that sense
I still feel like I'm 25

00:09:19.481 --> 00:09:22.598
when I was writing
Definitely Maybe and thinking

00:09:22.598 --> 00:09:25.060
that's just f-ing great,
you know, I just mastered

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.964
the art of writing my songs and I loved it

00:09:26.964 --> 00:09:29.083
and every time I finished
a song it was like, oh,

00:09:29.083 --> 00:09:32.862
another song, I still get that feeling,

00:09:32.862 --> 00:09:37.082
and as long as I don't lose
that, I'll always make music.

00:09:37.082 --> 00:09:39.903
- Thank you guys so much for
watching, I made this video

00:09:39.903 --> 00:09:43.676
because IGPK1987 asked me
to, so if there's a famous

00:09:43.676 --> 00:09:45.714
entrepreneur that you
want me to profile next,

00:09:45.714 --> 00:09:47.596
leave it down in the
comments below and I'll see

00:09:47.596 --> 00:09:49.777
what I can do, I'd also love
to see what did Noel say

00:09:49.777 --> 00:09:52.001
that had the biggest
impact on you and why,

00:09:52.001 --> 00:09:53.597
what would you take from this video

00:09:53.597 --> 00:09:56.221
and immediately apply
to your life or to your

00:09:56.221 --> 00:09:58.479
business somehow, please
leave it in the comments

00:09:58.479 --> 00:10:00.720
and I'm going to join in the
discussion, I also want to

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.601
give a quick shout-out to Reverse Stereo,

00:10:02.601 --> 00:10:04.359
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:10:04.359 --> 00:10:07.941
Your One Word, and making that
fun YouTube tribute video,

00:10:07.941 --> 00:10:10.977
I really, really appreciate your support.

00:10:10.977 --> 00:10:13.560
(upbeat music)

00:10:26.517 --> 00:10:27.916
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:10:27.916 --> 00:10:30.656
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:10:30.656 --> 00:10:34.823
and whatever your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

